{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Subprocess-environment research revised the prior strict-allowlist recommendation. Full inheritance is standard for ordinary trusted same-principal subprocesses and has not been community-rejected: Python subprocess defaults to inheritance; pip copies os.environ then offers targeted extra_environ/unset_environ; pre-commit defaults to inherited env; GitHub Actions overlays a step environment; Jenkins scopes credential env to a build block and descendants. Strict filtering appears at deliberate hermetic/reproducibility boundaries such as tox pass_env/disallow_pass_env and Bazel action/repository environments. A global or adapter allowlist in Rebar would be fragile across Git helpers, SSH agents, AWS credential_process, proxy/CA, runtime, and platform variables. The actual Rebar defect is sibling propagation: bridge reconciliation, ACLI, delivery, and detached push reuse broad parent environments. Revised unapproved candidate: preserve ambient inheritance for trusted same-capability children; never mutate global os.environ to install adapter secrets; overlay Rebar-materialized static values only onto their owning child; remove exact Rebar-declared secret variable names when launching an unrelated sibling; preserve unknown ambient/native chains; rely primarily on step/container/service secret scoping; defer strict/hermetic mode. Security claim is limited to Rebar-known credential non-propagation, not sandboxing. Research artifact updated; Gitleaks found no leaks."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786937929183152001,"uuid":"3d5ac8f3-390e-4bf3-8dfe-e4d62958b064"}